  "articlePanels": [{"kind":"lede","html":"<p>GoAudits is a good product. It is one of the better-value inspection apps in the UK, with strong templates and real traction. So the honest reason to look for a <strong>goaudits alternative uk</strong> teams will use is not that GoAudits is weak. It is that it is an app with a login, and for some teams that is the whole problem.</p><p>Here is a fair comparison, where GoAudits wins, and where a no-app approach wins.</p>"},{"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>GoAudits is a mature, well-priced inspection tool, from around £10 per seat per month, with UK-based support and real UK traction in construction, hospitality and retail.</li><li>If your team will open an app on site, GoAudits is a sensible, proven choice. We say that plainly.</li><li>The one thing it cannot change is that it is an app with a login. The same on-site adoption drop-off applies to any app.</li><li>Quickler's only real difference is no app. The workflow runs in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat.</li><li>Quickler is £20 per active user a month, dormant users free, only pay for who works, everything unlimited on a paid account.</li><li>Pricing figures for GoAudits are approximate. Check their current page before deciding.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Credit where due","heading":"What GoAudits does well","html":"<p>An honest comparison starts where the product is strong, and GoAudits is strong.</p><p>The template library is excellent and the builder is flexible enough to cover most inspection and audit types. The reporting is clean, PDF output is professional and configurable, and the dashboard gives a clear view of who has completed what across the team. Support is UK-based and responsive, which matters when you are mid-rollout.</p><p>The price is genuinely competitive, from around £10 per seat per month, which puts it well below the enterprise tools. For a UK firm that wants a capable inspection app without an enterprise bill, GoAudits is a reasonable default. None of that is in doubt.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The one catch","heading":"It is still an app with a login","html":"<p>The single structural limit is not a product flaw. It is the category. GoAudits is an app. Every worker downloads it and keeps a login alive. For a desk-based or adoption-tolerant team that is a non-issue.</p><p>For a team out on jobs, an app is one more thing to open when hands are full. What happens next is familiar. The inspection gets done. GoAudits gets filled in later, in the van or the car park, from memory. The record says completed. It reflects what someone remembered, not what they saw.</p><p>A cheaper app does not fix this, because the friction was never the price. It was the act of opening an app and logging in at the point of work. That is the honest reason some firms keep looking after trying GoAudits.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The alternatives","heading":"Apps, paper, and WhatsApp","html":"<p><strong>GoAudits</strong> is the app-based option done well: good templates, clean reports, UK support, low price. Pick it when the team will use an app. See our <a href=\"/pages/iauditor-alternative-uk.html\">iAuditor alternative</a> for the wider app-market teardown.</p><p><strong>Paper and spreadsheets</strong> stay common in UK teams. Free, familiar, no training. The records are illegible, unsearchable, and impossible to produce when a client or regulator asks. The cost is not the subscription. It is the liability when the folder cannot be found.</p><p><strong>Quickler</strong> removes the app. The workflow arrives in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat and they reply to each question as a message. Same behaviour they already do all day, no install, no login. The tradeoff is less template flexibility than a dedicated form builder.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The no-app option","heading":"Quickler, and where it stops","html":"<p>Quickler runs in WhatsApp. Nothing to install, no login. The inspection workflow arrives in the worker's existing WhatsApp chat, and they reply to each question as a message. Voice notes are transcribed automatically. The core output is a shareable web report and a permanent record, with optional PDF and CSV export. Pricing is only for active users: [[price:bundles]]. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so you add your whole team and only pay for who actually works. Everything is unlimited on a paid account, so anyone who reads but never files adds nothing to the cost.</p><p>The honest limit: quickler is not a drag-and-drop form builder. It does not have thousands of pre-built templates, and for complex branching audits with sophisticated scoring logic, GoAudits is the more flexible product. Quickler is built for teams of roughly 1 to 30 doing structured compliance and inspection capture. Rooted in UK compliance, it works anywhere WhatsApp does. If your team will happily use an app, GoAudits is a mature choice and there is no reason to switch. If adoption is the problem, the no-app approach is the thing to test.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Price, install, support","heading":"Direct comparison","html":"<table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Pricing model</th><th>App install?</th><th>Support</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>GoAudits</td><td>Per seat, from ~£10/seat/mo</td><td>Yes, login required</td><td>UK-focused team</td></tr><tr><td>iAuditor</td><td>Per seat, from ~£19/seat/mo</td><td>Yes, login required</td><td>Global, UK timezone</td></tr><tr><td>Lumiform</td><td>Per seat, from ~£12/seat/mo</td><td>Yes, login required</td><td>European HQ</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Quickler</strong></td><td><strong>£20 per active user/mo, dormant users free</strong></td><td><strong>No, WhatsApp only</strong></td><td><strong>UK-based, direct founder</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Pricing is approximate. Check each vendor's current pricing before deciding. The deciding test is simple: will your team open an app on site, or not. If yes, GoAudits is a good, cheaper answer. If not, a WhatsApp-native workflow removes the barrier because they already use WhatsApp for everything else. One thing to check before switching: does the alternative produce a report format your clients and regulators will accept? PDF output from any reputable tool is usually fine, but verify if your sector has specific requirements. See <a href=\"/pages/safetyculture-alternative-uk.html\">SafetyCulture alternative UK</a> and <a href=\"/pages/lumiform-alternative-uk.html\">Lumiform alternative UK</a>.</p>"},{"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>Is GoAudits any good?</h3><p>Yes. GoAudits is a mature, well-priced inspection app with strong templates, clean reporting and UK-based support. If your team will use an app, it is a sensible, proven choice. We would not tell you otherwise.</p><h3>What is the difference between GoAudits and Quickler?</h3><p>GoAudits is a dedicated inspection app: install, log in, complete forms inside it. Quickler runs in WhatsApp: the worker receives questions in their existing chat and replies. GoAudits has a larger template library and a more flexible form builder. Quickler has higher on-site completion because there is no app to open and no login to remember.</p><h3>Is Quickler cheaper than GoAudits?</h3><p>It depends on your team shape. GoAudits charges per seat, from around £10 a seat a month. Quickler charges £20 per active user a month, where an active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, and dormant users are free. A team with many occasional users often pays less on quickler. A small team where everyone is active every month may pay less on GoAudits. Check both against your own numbers.</p><h3>Should I switch from GoAudits to Quickler?</h3><p>Only if adoption is your problem. If your team happily opens the GoAudits app on site, stay put. If people fill forms later from memory because opening an app is one step too many, test a WhatsApp-native workflow. Quickler has a free tier of 20 reports a month, up to 10 photos per report, no card and no trial clock, so you can try it without committing. Related: <a href=\"/pages/lumiform-alternative-uk.html\">Lumiform alternative UK</a> and <a href=\"/pages/alternatives.html\">all comparisons</a>.</p>"}],
